<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyaukse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyaukse is a town and district south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> in central Myanmar, on a plain watered by the Zawgyi and Panlaung rivers. Its weirs and canals, among the oldest irrigation works in the country, made it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> granary of the dry zone, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> who settled there in the 9th century drew on it to build the Pagan Kingdom. Control of Kyaukse remained a key to power for the later kingdoms of Myinsaing, Pinya and Ava.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyaukse.jpg" alt="Kyaukse" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekyaw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown,_Kyaukse,_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kyaukse lies on the Zawgyi River, 25 miles (40 km) south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, and is the capital of Kyaukse District in Mandalay Region. The surrounding land is a level strip running south from Mandalay along the foothills of the Shan Plateau. Although it lies in the heart of the dry zone, it is drained by the Panlaung and Zawgyi rivers, whose ancient irrigation canals predate the settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> in the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Local inscriptions of the 12th and 13th centuries call Kyaukse the first home. Under the Pagan kings the plain was known as the Eleven Agricultural Districts, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> built nine fortresses along its rivers, among them Tamote, against invasion by water. The name, meaning stone dam, is usually traced to stone-reinforced embankments on the Zawgyi River, though another view links it to the Min Ye Dam of King Narapati Sithu (r. 1174–1211). By tradition Anawrahta's white elephant chose the site of the Shwethalyaung Pagoda for relics he brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and an elephant dance festival at the foot of the hill honours the royal elephants.</p><p>Under the Konbaung dynasty (1752–1885) the Nine Districts of Kyaukse were an administrative and agricultural hub, ruled from the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> (r. 1782–1819) by a royal governor, the Kyaukse wun; the last recorded, U Pe Gyi, served under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">Thibaw</a> before the British annexation. The historian Gordon Luce argued that Burmese civilisation began in the Kyaukse irrigation zone, settled by groups from Nanzhao, a view that Htin Aung and other Burmese historians rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kyaukse lies on the Mandalay-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> railway, and its township has one town, 10 wards and 223 villages. A heavy industrial zone was established there under the military government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/than-shwe">Than Shwe</a>, whose home town it is, and the area is known for its turmeric, mangoes and onions. In the 2025 Myanmar earthquake Kyaukse was among the most damaged towns, with more than 170 people reported dead and 732 injured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyaukse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most people were rice farmers; the irrigated lands of <b>Kyaukse</b> and Minbu fed the court, while the delta, depopulated by the wars of the 1750s, was gradually resettled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The newcomers settled first on the irrigated plain of <b>Kyaukse</b> and then along the middle Irrawaddy, and the Burmese chronicles date the fortification of Pagan, on a bend of the river, to 849 under a king named Pyinbya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities rose in the three great irrigated regions of Upper Burma: the Mu valley, the <b>Kyaukse</b> plains and the Minbu region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s economic heart was the irrigated rice land of Upper Burma, above all the <b>Kyaukse</b> district and the Minbu and Mu valley areas, which fed the capital and the royal service population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ava controlled the <b>Kyaukse</b> and Minbu rice lands and claimed authority over Prome, Toungoo and the Shan borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Athinkhaya, Yazathingyan and Thihathu, sons of a Shan chief and commanders under the last Pagan kings, built up power in the <b>Kyaukse</b> district from bases at Myinsaing, Mekkaya and Pinle.</blockquote><a href="/state/myinsaing-kingdom">Myinsaing Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
